Z||000000||Slammed Fiero||09-12-2001||11:58 PM||||These pics are big , I did this with Dialup so I know how bad it sucks to load.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/brakes_3.JPG">  <P>11.25" Vented Discs , TR Motorsports Typhoons 17" and Pirelli P Zero "C" R compound rubber.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/back1.JPG">  <BR>My Fiero and My dads 94 911 RS America.<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Bottle.JPG">  <BR>NOS Bottle with remote opener<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dash.JPG">  <BR>New white face gauges , yes I know the stop pin on the tach is missing. <P> <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.<p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Slammed Fiero||09-13-2001||12:07 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/switch.JPG">  <BR>Hidden under Ashtray Door. NOS switch and "valet" key. I can turn the key and lock the nos out completley. The stitch plate is made from plexiglass. All fits perfectly  under door away from prying eyes. The Lock is from an old computer. Easy to wire in. <P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/side1.JPG">  <BR>Side shot. <P><BR>Thats all Folks!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Leper||09-13-2001||12:09 AM||||You weren't kidding about the pics. Sheesh!<BR>Looks great though.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Adrift||09-13-2001||12:11 AM||||Did you use a kit for the big breaks? Where did you buy them from? How much $$?<BR>Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||Slammed Fiero||09-13-2001||12:18 AM||||No kit used for the brakes. Archie sells a Kit for $1600 I believe. The caliper Mounting brackets alone are $450 Canadian. Plus all the hardware. you can go cheap and use  used parts such as calipers , and caliper bolts , cheap pads. I used all new parts , hi quality steet/ track pads , new caliper bolts..ect..ect. The cost is in the machining. The bore of the front rotors has to be reduced. You can do this 2 ways , have them weld in a bead of machinable weld into the bore and machine it to fit the hub , or do what I did and have an insert machined up and tacked in , then have the rotor balanced. The rear bores have to be enlarged. For the front you need spacers behind the new caliper brackets. I believe there .25" thick , mine are stainless , the info I got said to use washers. You can skip the blazer master cylinder and save a few bucks.<P>This is not an undertaking for an amatuer , nor is it a $500 brake conversion. If you want to stop better and spend a lot less go with the grand am conversion.<P>As for cost... Not sure at this point , but archies cost is pretty close to what I have in the conversion , less a few personal touches that aren't necessary. This winter I will get around to putting the Wilwoods on the front (4 piston) or I have been looking at 911 Front calipers.<P>JM<p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||09-13-2001||05:14 AM||||Slammed...send me some pics and fill out the form for my book...please?!<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/amsig.gif"> <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A>||||reg||1
