Z||000010||VISCERAL||09-13-2001||11:13 AM||||Hi, you are using wilwoods, right? what is the part # for the front caliper? I have the same parts, but haven't thrown it together. Do you have to do what you said to the front rotor, can't it just slide on or will it rattle loosely?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Slammed Fiero||09-13-2001||12:07 PM||||The front rotor requires the insert to fit snugly onto the hub. I wouldn't want to try it without. An alternative may be to get the insert putonto the hub , welded and balanced. That way you can just swap rotors when you wear them out.<P>The problem with wilwoods Vs Camaro Calipers is  that a camaro caliper is a floating design , meaning it moves on the caliper bolts to provide the clamping action required to stop the car.<P>The wilwoods are a Fixed design. Meaning both sides of the caliper have pistons and  clamp inward. This requires a bit of spacing and engineering. The calipers I have a Dynalite 2. I just called them up with my brake rotor specs and they mailed them to me.<P>Now...I personally would try it with the Camaro calipers first. I was Amazed at how well this car stopped. I did several 100mph + stops where I stood on the brake pedal and the car didn't show any sign of fade.<P>I'm going to look into porsche 911 calipers (4 piston) or 944 / 951 calipers. These come up on ebay every so often for under $100 a set and I have friends who can rebuild them if need be.<P>For now I wanted to get the car on the road. I finished it Wednesday night at 5:30 and took off on a 4 hr trip to see my Girlfriend the next morning.<P>I will keep you posted on the 4 piston conversion. I'm skeptical as to whether or not the Wilwoods will be that much better. You should see the size of the piston on the Camaro caliper Vs. The stock Fiero calipers.<P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||09-13-2001||01:34 PM||||hows well does it work? can ya keep up with the RS-A?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> is that the factory paint? looks flawless!?! what jetting are you using? ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Slammed Fiero||09-13-2001||04:19 PM||||Braking wise I can keep up with the RS <P>Acceleration...the RS-A was turning 13.2's stock , this was before the Motec Brain kit , B&B Triflow headers and exhaust , tuning ect. So even on the bottle I would get whooped. The previous owner of the car went nuts with mods , it has a factory 40% LSD , deeper gears , lightened flywheel , Sachs Race clutch, coilovers , Koni Yellow's adjustable bars. A real precision race machine that really doesn't belong on the street.<P>As for the jetting..it's a 70hp shot. Not too sure what it is but perhaps Sluppy will read this andtell me what he sold me. I believe a 36/42 is whats in there.<P>I have yet to take it to the strip. Hoping for 13's.<P>The paint is original with the exception of the nose , 1 fender and the hood. The car currently has in the neighbourhood of 48,000 miles.<P>Next project is an interior redo and some sparco seats.||||reg||1
Z||000014||sluppy123||09-13-2001||04:58 PM||||The Jets were the ones that came with the kit, I think is was 36 N2O and a 42 bypass.<P>Those brakes look badA$$, just what I need. Have you tried the NOS yet?  <P>------------------<BR>-Brian, Toolmaker<BR>Lincoln, NE<BR>87' GT-Galaxy silver mettalic, Turbo Charged, Modified 2.8- 235 HP w/ 7 psi Boost, Eibach Sport springs, RSM side scoops, Mr Mikes leather, 17" Mille Miglia's. <BR>               <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1711832&a=12781563&p=48213929&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
